ADS(1)                           User Commands                          ADS(1)

NAME
       ads - Advanced Dada System (Terminal Analytics Platform)

SYNOPSIS
       ads <command> [arguments] [flags]

DESCRIPTION
       ads is a decentralized, CLI-first terminal session management and 
       analytics platform. It eschews traditional background daemons and 
       custom PTY emulators in favor of utilizing the native tmux and ssh 
       binaries. All terminal output is captured, stripped of ANSI control 
       sequences, and dual-written to SQLite databases for high-performance 
       Full-Text Search (FTS) and semantic command analysis.

COMMANDS
       new [name]
           Provisions a new tracking database for a session.
           Flags:
             -r, --remote    Create a remote session. Prompts the user 
                             interactively for SSH User, Host, and Port.
             -p, --profile   Specifies the Tmux Profile to utilize 
                             (defaults to 'default'). Profiles are stored 
                             as renderable templates within the Meta-Database.

       run [name]
           Executes and attaches to the specified session.
           The orchestrator dynamically fetches the assigned Tmux Profile 
           from the Meta-Database, renders it to an ephemeral config file 
           in /tmp, and spawns the local multiplexer.
           
           If the session is local, it spawns a bash shell. If remote, it 
           securely initiates an ssh connection inside the tmux pane. 
           Terminal output is piped natively to the ads-recorder background 
           binary. Re-running on a detached session will reattach instantly.

       list
           Displays a tabulated index of all known sessions, showing their 
           Name, UUID, Type (local/remote), Status, and Creation Date.

       search [query]
           Executes a federated FTS5 search across all recorded session 
           databases. Returns highlighted snippets of terminal output 
           matching the query, annotated with the origin session name.

       auth test [name]
           Headless utility to verify SSH connectivity for a remote session 
           prior to execution. Utilizes BatchMode to strictly test the 
           native ssh-agent credentials without prompting for passwords.

       delete [name]
           Permanently unlinks the session's SQLite .db file from the local 
           filesystem and drops the session record from the Meta-Database.

       hook [shell]
           Generates shell integration scripts to enable semantic chunking.
           Currently supports: bash
           Example usage: eval "$(ads hook bash)"
           Injects invisible OSC 133 sequences into the terminal to boundary-
           mark prompts, user input, and command executions for the database.

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
       USER
           Used as the default SSH username when provisioning a remote session 
           via 'ads new --remote'.

DEFAULT TMUX INTEGRATIONS
       By default, the 'default' tmux profile automatically injects key 
       workflow optimizations:
           * Scrollback history is increased to 100,000 lines.
           * Mouse support is globally enabled.
           * Prefix + s opens an interactive command prompt that natively 
             executes an 'ads search' query across all session databases, 
             displaying the results in a split pane via 'less'.

FILES
       ~/.local/share/ads/meta.db
           The central orchestrator database storing session metadata, 
           remote configurations, and statuses.

       ~/.local/share/ads/sessions/<uuid>.db
           The isolated, per-session Write-Ahead Log (WAL) SQLite databases 
           storing the raw io_stream, the sanitized fts_index, and the 
           semantic command_history.

EXAMPLES
       Create and run a local session:
           $ ads new my-local-task
           $ ads run my-local-task

       Create, test, and run a remote SSH session:
           $ ads new my-server --remote
           # (Prompts for User, Host, Port)
           $ ads auth test my-server
           $ ads run my-server

       Search for an error across all past sessions:
           $ ads search "Out of memory"

       Enable semantic tracking in your current shell:
           $ eval "$(ads hook bash)"

